<Project>

  <!--
    Directory.Build.targets - build-time enforcement.

    The guardrail script is wired to the API project's build rather than to
    every project's build, so a full solution build runs it exactly once
    instead of eleven times.

    This is a safety net, not the primary mechanism. The primary mechanism is
    the git pre-commit hook installed by scripts/install-hooks.ps1, which
    catches violations before they can be committed at all.

    ========================================================================
    STATUS AS OF P1-02b (2026-08-16): THIS TARGET IS LIVE. IT RUNS ON EVERY
    BUILD. THE BEHAVIOUR BELOW WAS MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED.
    ========================================================================

      dotnet build, full solution         guardrails run
      dotnet build, nothing changed       guardrails run. The target has no
                                          Inputs or Outputs, so MSBuild
                                          never skips it as up to date, even
                                          when compilation itself is skipped.
      dotnet build with
        MerchSkipGuardrails=true          guardrails skipped, as designed

    It fired for the first time on P1-01's build, and did not misfire. It
    ran four more times during P1-02 without being disturbed by that card
    converting the API to the Web SDK: the Condition keys on the project
    NAME, not on which SDK the project uses.

    WHAT THIS HEADER USED TO SAY, AND WHY IT CHANGED. Until P1-02b it read
    "THIS TARGET IS DEAD CODE. IT HAS NEVER RUN." That was true when written
    at P0-07, when src/ held only .gitkeep and no build had ever run in this
    repository. It stopped being true at P1-01, which created the project
    and built it, and the header was not updated with it. P1-02 found the
    contradiction while reading this file for an unrelated reason.

    IT STILL DOES NOT REPLACE THE GIT PRE-COMMIT HOOK. That conclusion in
    CLAUDE.md section 11 stands, but its REASON has changed, and the new
    reason is weaker than the old one. Read the difference carefully:

      OLD REASON   this target never runs, so it protects nothing.
      REAL REASON  this target runs only when a BUILD runs. An edit made in
                   Visual Studio and committed without a build is invisible
                   to it, as is any commit that does not rebuild the API
                   project. MerchSkipGuardrails=true bypasses it outright.

    So it is a safety net that catches a violation at the moment you build,
    which is usually earlier than commit time. It is NOT a gate. The gate is
    the git pre-commit hook (L4), and deleting that hook on the strength of
    this target would reopen precisely the Visual-Studio-edit hole L4 exists
    to cover.

    On the interpreter: this deliberately invokes 'powershell' (Windows
    PowerShell 5.1) rather than 'pwsh'. pwsh is roughly 3x faster and is what
    the Claude Code hooks use, but it is not guaranteed present on a machine
    that only has Visual Studio installed. This target runs once per solution
    build, so correctness on an unprepared machine beats ~1.2 seconds.
  -->

  <Target Name="MerchGuardrails"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Merchandising.Api' AND '$(MerchSkipGuardrails)' != 'true'">

    <PropertyGroup>
      <MerchGuardrailScript>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)scripts\check-no-csharp.ps1</MerchGuardrailScript>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Exec Command="powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;$(MerchGuardrailScript)&quot;"
          Condition="Exists('$(MerchGuardrailScript)')"
          ContinueOnError="false" />

  </Target>

</Project>
